Full Deploy Flow Summary — DevOps Central Platform
1. Infrastructure Provisioning (Terraform + Libvirt)
No Makefile, Vagrantfile, or docker-compose exists at root. Infrastructure is provisioned in two layers:
Terraform (terraform/main.tf) provisions VMs on a libvirt/KVM hypervisor:
- Provider: dmacvicar/libvirt ~> 0.9 talking to qemu:///system.
- Creates a private NAT network (libvirt_network) with configurable CIDR (default 192.168.56.0/24), DNS forwarders (1.1.1.1, 8.8.8.8), DHCP range.
- Clones an Ubuntu 22.04 cloud image into per-node qcow2 volumes.
- Provisions 1 master + N workers (default 2, range 1-32) with configurable vCPU (2), RAM (2048 MB), disk (20 GB).
- Each VM gets a cloud-init ISO with:
- Hardened SSH (ssh_pwauth: false, disable_root: true, PermitRootLogin no, restricted sudo for kubeadm/kubelet/containerd only, fail2ban installed).
- Static IP from CIDR (master at .10, workers at .11+).
- Automatic disk grow/fs resize.
- Ansible inventory auto-generated (local_file.ansible_inventory → terraform/inventory.ini).
- State backend: currently local (backend.tf documents S3 migration path). All *.tfstate* gitignored (P0 #4).
Key variables (variables.tf): ssh_public_key marked sensitive = true (P0 #4), all outputs marked sensitive (P0 #4). Validations enforce RFC1918 CIDR, worker count 1-32, vCPU 1-16, memory 512-32768 MB, disk 8-1024 GB.
2. Kubernetes Bootstrapping (Ansible)
Ansible (ansible/playbook.yml) runs against the Terraform-provisioned VMs using the generated inventory. Galaxy-compliant roles with per-role defaults/main.yml + meta/main.yml.
Steps (tags gated):
